A queue gives a level-order search. It is more work than following left or right links in a BST, but it is easy to trace and returns the matching node without changing the tree.

```csharp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     public int val;
 *     public TreeNode left;
 *     public TreeNode right;
 *     public TreeNode(int val=0, TreeNode left=null, TreeNode right=null) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */

using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public TreeNode searchBST(TreeNode root, int val) {
        if (root == null) {
            return null;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new Queue<TreeNode>();
        queue.Enqueue(root);

        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            TreeNode node = queue.Dequeue();

            if (node.val == val) {
                return node;
            }

            if (node.left != null) {
                queue.Enqueue(node.left);
            }
            if (node.right != null) {
                queue.Enqueue(node.right);
            }
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```

For a tree rooted at `8`, with children `3` and `10`, a search for `10` checks `8`, then the next level, and returns the right child. The empty tree and a missing value both finish with `null`.

This uses more memory than a direct BST walk and does not take advantage of the ordering. It is still a valid lookup for the given tree.

Time complexity is `O(n)`. Space complexity is `O(w)`, where `w` is the largest number of nodes in one level.

A recursive version follows exactly one branch at each step. An empty root means the value is not present. When the current node matches, returning that node also returns its whole subtree, which is the format expected here.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public TreeNode searchBST(TreeNode root, int val) {
        if (root == null || root.data == val) {
            return root;
        }

        if (val < root.data) {
            return searchBST(root.left, val);
        }

        return searchBST(root.right, val);
    }
}
```

The empty-tree case and a missing value both return `null`. Recursion keeps the code small, but a very unbalanced tree can create a deep call stack.

Time complexity is `O(h)`. Space complexity is `O(h)` because of the recursive calls.

Here the stack keeps a normal depth-first search. The BST direction is still checked, but the code also safely handles a tree that is not perfectly shaped. Once a node has the requested value, the node pointer is returned.

```go
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     data int
 *     left *TreeNode
 *     right *TreeNode
 * }
 **/

func searchBST(root *TreeNode, val int) *TreeNode {
    if root == nil {
        return nil
    }

    stack := []*TreeNode{root}

    for len(stack) > 0 {
        last := len(stack) - 1
        node := stack[last]
        stack = stack[:last]

        if node.data == val {
            return node
        }

        if node.right != nil {
            stack = append(stack, node.right)
        }
        if node.left != nil {
            stack = append(stack, node.left)
        }
    }

    return nil
}
```

The important edge cases are an empty tree and a value that does not occur. Both return `nil`. Since this is a full depth-first scan, it does not depend on the BST ordering as strongly as the direct branch search.

Time complexity is `O(n)` in the worst case. The stack uses `O(h)` space for a tree-shaped search, and can be `O(n)` for a very wide tree.

The tree property makes the search short. If the current value is smaller than `val`, only the right side can work. If it is larger, move left. The loop stops when it finds the node or reaches an empty link.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
public:
    TreeNode* searchBST(TreeNode* root, int val) {
        TreeNode* current = root;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            if (current->data == val) {
                return current;
            }

            if (val < current->data) {
                current = current->left;
            } else {
                current = current->right;
            }
        }

        return nullptr;
    }
};
```

For example, in a tree with values `8, 3, 10, 1, 6`, searching for `6` checks `8`, then `3`, then reaches `6`.

Time complexity is `O(h)`, where `h` is the tree height. Space complexity is `O(1)`.

